
# Context & Working Agreement

## Project
Nx monorepo called `jobber` (NestJS microservices), built by following Michael
Guay's Udemy course. Current app: `jobber-auth` under `apps/`.
Stack: Nx, NestJS 11, Prisma 7, PostgreSQL (via docker-compose), Node >= 22.12.

## The single most important thing to understand
I follow the course's VIDEO lessons, but the author's video code and his GitHub
repo DIVERGE:
- The **videos** demonstrate an older **Prisma 6-style** workflow (client output
  into `node_modules`, bare imports, `extends PrismaClient` + `$connect()` with
  NO driver adapter, `url` inside `schema.prisma`).
- His **current GitHub repo is Prisma 7** (driver adapters, `prisma-client`
  generator, client generated into the source tree, no `url` in schema).

Therefore: **the author's GitHub repo is the source of truth, NOT the video.**
Repo: https://github.com/mguay22/jobber
Read his files via raw URLs, e.g.:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mguay22/jobber/main/<path>

## I am on NEWER tooling than the author — adapt, don't copy blindly
| Tool               | Author  | Me       |
|--------------------|---------|----------|
| TypeScript         | ~5.9.3  | ~6.0.3   |
| Nx                 | 22.3.3  | 23.1.0   |
| @prisma/adapter-pg | ^7.2.0  | ^7.9.0   |
| @types/node        | ~25     | ^22      |

Because of this, some of his files need small adjustments for me (e.g. TS 6
deprecates `baseUrl` -> TS5101; stricter module resolution). Never suggest a
change that only works on his older versions.

## Prisma 7 setup already in place — DO NOT revert these
- `schema.prisma`: generator `provider = "prisma-client"`, `moduleFormat = "cjs"`,
  `importFileExtension = ""`, output into the source tree; datasource has NO `url`.
- `prisma.config.ts`: holds the datasource `url` from `process.env.AUTH_DATABASE_URL`
  (NOT prisma's strict `env()` helper), and loads `.env` via a guarded
  `process.loadEnvFile()` (tolerates a missing `.env` in CI).
- `PrismaService`: `super({ adapter: new PrismaPg({ connectionString:
  process.env.AUTH_DATABASE_URL }) })`; import PrismaClient via a RELATIVE path
  to the generated folder (not a bare specifier, not `node_modules`).
- `main.ts`: guarded `process.loadEnvFile()` before bootstrap.
- `project.json`: `build` and `test` both `dependsOn: ["generate-types"]`;
  `migrate-prisma` runs `prisma migrate dev --name {args.name}` (default `init`).
- The generated Prisma client is git-ignored and regenerated by `generate-types`.
- CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs `nx affected -t lint test build` and must
  work on a clean checkout (no `.env`, no generated client).

## What to do when the video and reality disagree
When I paste an error (or say "the video said X but it fails"):
1. Identify the relevant file(s) in MY local repo.
2. Fetch the corresponding file(s) from the author's GitHub repo (raw URL).
3. Diff three things: what the video showed, what the author's repo has now, and
   what my local code has.
4. Recommend the fix that matches the author's Prisma 7 ARCHITECTURE but is
   ADAPTED to my newer tooling. Explain *why* it broke (which version/feature),
   not just the fix.
5. Never undo the Prisma 7 adaptations listed above.
6. Prefer minimal, surgical changes. If a fix is environmental (Node version,
   docker-compose creds, missing `.env`), say so instead of changing code.

When you need info, ask me for the exact error text and which lesson/feature
I'm on. Assume the author's repo may be AHEAD of my current lesson, so only apply
what's relevant to where I am.

## Keep this file updated
As the course progresses, add newly-resolved breaking changes to the
"DO NOT revert" list so a fresh LLM session doesn't roll my code back to the
video's older style.
